WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:03.330
How do you teach a parrot to dance?

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:05.790
Or a cockroach to hit its mark?

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.160
Well, Hollywood has a range of tricks

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.410
to train these stars

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:12.301
and every other kind of
insect and animal imaginable.

00:00:12.301 --> 00:00:13.150
Trainers: Come on, baby!

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:14.400
Right here, come on!

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.870
Narrator: From penguins to house spiders,

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:19.940
let's look at how 10 different
members of the animal kingdom

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.207
prepare for some of
Hollywood's biggest roles.

00:00:23.207 --> 00:00:25.082
Scout Raskin: Good boy!

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.190
Narrator: These goats,
named Gizmo and Doc,

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:31.681
can shake hooves, give high fives,

00:00:31.681 --> 00:00:33.964
jump on a person, balance on their back,

00:00:33.964 --> 00:00:36.100
and even walk on their hind legs.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.300
Scout: Walk, walk, walk, walk, walk.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.810
Narrator: Their trainer, Scout Raskin,

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:40.818
says they're just as smart as dogs,

00:00:40.818 --> 00:00:43.270
and maybe even smarter in one area:

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:44.740
object permanence.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.155
That's the ability to know

00:00:46.155 --> 00:00:48.246
that an object continues to exist

00:00:48.246 --> 00:00:50.113
even if it disappears from your view.

00:00:50.113 --> 00:00:52.840
If Scout needs a goat to go
to a certain spot on set,

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.270
she'll hide treats in that
area and show the goat,

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:57.500
but not let it enjoy them just yet.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.550
Then, when the director calls "action,"

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.670
the goat will go right
to the hidden treats.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:03.800
Not because it's sniffing
them out like a dog would,

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.470
but because it remembers
the treats are there.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:09.280
Other tricks rely on
goats' natural movements.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.010
Like in the horror movie "The Witch,"

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.340
where this goat bounces sideways --

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.120
that movement is actually
an expression of joy.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.443
And getting a goat to
rear on its hind legs,

00:01:18.443 --> 00:01:19.557
like in this scene,

00:01:19.557 --> 00:01:22.987
mimics how goats naturally
reach leaves on tree branches.

00:01:22.987 --> 00:01:25.550
Scout usually uses a
grabber tool with a treat

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:27.490
to have the goat get up on its hind legs.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:30.640
Then, slowly, she adds
in two-legged walking.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.870
But these animals can also be dangerous.

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.610
On the fourth day of filming "The Witch,"

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:37.660
Charlie, who played Black Phillip,

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:41.170
rammed his horns into
actor Ralph Ineson's ribs,

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:42.830
something that happened in the movie

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.619
but wasn't supposed to
happen in real life.

00:01:45.619 --> 00:01:47.870
Ineson spent the rest
of the five-week shoot

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.270
on pain killers.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:50.940
The incident led to the movie's director,

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.957
Robert Eggers, to say after filming,

00:01:52.957 --> 00:01:55.210
"I don't recommend making
a movie with a goat

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:56.220
in a major role,"

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.350
and, "You can't train a goat."

00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:59.700
You can, Scout says,

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.340
but you have to start when
the goat is very young,

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:03.640
less than two months old,

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.915
and practice nearly every day.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.700
A lot of people don't like spiders --

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:12.550
Can we panic now?

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:14.160
Narrator: Which makes
them a great character

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.350
in Hollywood movies.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:16.560
But how do you get these critters

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.320
to do what you want on screen?

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.700
You get them to react to
stimuli in predictable ways,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.130
according to the
entomologist Steven Kutcher.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.770
Steven is Hollywood's go-to bug guy,

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:29.060
who's worked on films from "Jurassic Park"

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.078
to "Spider-Man" and orchestrated

00:02:31.078 --> 00:02:33.290
this spider attack in "Arachnophobia."

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:36.360
For that movie, he got the
spiders to run where he wanted

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.890
by stringing extremely
thin wires on the set.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.630
These wires vibrated faster
than the eye can see,

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.640
forming lanes that the
spiders would run through.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.430
For this pivotal "Spider-Man" scene,

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.740
Steven staged a "spider Olympics"

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.830
to find the right actor for the part.

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:54.185
He settled on this type of house spider

00:02:54.185 --> 00:02:56.058
and had an artist paint its body

00:02:56.058 --> 00:02:57.900
radioactive red and blue.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:58.980
When they shot the scene,

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.830
Steven was above Tobey Maguire

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.140
holding the spider on a paintbrush.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.173
It crawled along the edge of the brush,

00:03:05.173 --> 00:03:06.540
and when it reached the end,

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.923
Steven tapped the brush to make the spider

00:03:08.923 --> 00:03:10.930
drop down towards Tobey.

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:13.116
Only the bite was CGI.

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.640
A peppered cockroach named Nancy

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.880
played a roach with the voice of God

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.840
in the horror movie "Saint Maud."

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:24.840
But Nancy couldn't have
pulled off the role

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.210
without her trainer, Grace Dickinson.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.980
Here, Grace is holding Pepper,

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.670
Nancy's sister who served
as her acting double

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:33.050
for "Saint Maud."

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.720
Grace says cockroaches are scent-oriented

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.310
and naturally explore
the places they're in.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:40.290
Before a roach's big filming day,

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.330
she'll typically practice
leading the insect

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:44.130
from point A to point B

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.960
by repeatedly guiding it down a path

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.730
that ends in something
sweet, like a pot of jelly

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:51.180
or a sip of nectar or honey water.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.900
Grace says cockroaches in general

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:54.730
have naturally even temperaments,

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:56.430
but since they're small creatures,

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:59.600
sudden light or temperature
changes can stress them out.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.760
If a roach performer is having a bad day,

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.490
it might make hissing noises,

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:04.681
drop a stink bomb,

00:04:04.681 --> 00:04:06.906
or try to fly off or run away.

00:04:06.906 --> 00:04:09.470
So Grace brings a rigged habitat on set,

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:11.810
where her actors can get
some much-needed rest

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:13.743
between filming sessions.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.740
In battle scenes from older
movies, like this one,

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:21.770
you may see horses
falling head over heels.

00:04:21.770 --> 00:04:23.286
That's because battlefield sets

00:04:23.286 --> 00:04:25.270
were lined with trip wires,

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:28.790
a dangerous and inhumane
practice that's now illegal.

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:31.170
Today, horses are trained
to fall to the ground

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.300
in battle scenes, and to do it safely.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.140
First, the horse has to get comfortable

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:37.570
lying down on its side.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:39.560
Some trainers will help this process along

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.490
by first lifting their horse's
legs and laying them down.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:44.890
Then they'll walk the
horse and lay them down

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:47.920
over and over until the
horse can do it alone.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.400
Eventually, the horse will
be able to lie down on cue

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.380
in one fluid motion.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.820
The faster they do it, the
more dangerous it gets,

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:56.438
so trainers will usually make sure

00:04:56.438 --> 00:04:58.542
the horses land on a cushioned bed,

00:04:58.542 --> 00:05:01.650
like two feet of soft,
natural material called peat,

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.680
or, in the case of "Game of
Thrones," padded mattresses.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:07.260
Productions use padding
in chase scenes as well,

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.920
like this horse and motorcycle chase

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:10.125
in "John Wick 3."

00:05:10.125 --> 00:05:12.950
Trainers laid 400 feet of rubber tarps

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:14.360
on the streets of New York City

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.480
and fitted the horses' hooves
with rubber horseshoes.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.530
This helped the horses maintain balance,

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:22.363
so they'd feel comfortable
galloping at full speed.

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:26.810
When it comes to training birds,

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:28.917
not all species are created equal.

00:05:28.917 --> 00:05:30.592
[clicker clicks]
Benay Karp: Good boy.

00:05:30.592 --> 00:05:33.327
Narrator: Of the 3,200 trained birds

00:05:33.327 --> 00:05:34.590
used in "The Birds,"

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:37.380
Alfred Hitchcock said the
ravens were the cleverest,

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:39.830
while the seagulls were the most vicious.

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:42.521
Back in 1963, the seagulls on the film

00:05:42.521 --> 00:05:45.540
were reportedly fed a
mixture of wheat and whiskey

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.650
to get them to stand around for scenes.

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:50.986
Luckily, training approaches
have improved since then.

00:05:50.986 --> 00:05:52.830
Hope you folks figure this thing out!

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:54.620
Narrator: Here, a grey parrot named Babs

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:55.986
is learning how to dance.

00:05:55.986 --> 00:05:56.819
[clicker clicks]

00:05:56.819 --> 00:05:57.890
Benay: Good bird.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.033
Narrator: The thing making that sound --

00:05:59.033 --> 00:05:59.866
[clicker clicks]

00:05:59.866 --> 00:06:00.699
is a clicker,

00:06:00.699 --> 00:06:02.250
a tool trainer Benay Karp

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.760
uses to provide birds
with instant feedback

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:06.660
after they do something correctly.

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.269
Benay teaches her parrots to be

00:06:08.269 --> 00:06:10.287
as quiet as possible on set

00:06:10.287 --> 00:06:12.750
so they don't step on other actors' lines,

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.720
rewarding good behavior with treats

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:16.073
like frozen strawberries.

00:06:16.073 --> 00:06:16.906
Parrot: Thank you!

00:06:16.906 --> 00:06:18.470
Narrator: Often, though,
productions want parrots

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:20.500
to look like they're talking in a scene.

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:22.675
In that case, Benay will
put a little peanut butter

00:06:22.675 --> 00:06:23.810
in the parrot's mouth.

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:25.590
As the bird smacks on the peanut butter,

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.540
it looks like delivering dialogue,

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:30.110
so the production can put
in voiceover later on.

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:31.809
Parrot: Enhanced voice recognition.

00:06:31.809 --> 00:06:33.640
Narrator: One of the most
common production requests

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:37.280
for birds is a basic flight
from point A to point B.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:38.920
Or, in the case of this penguin,

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:39.753
a waddle.

00:06:39.753 --> 00:06:41.419
Trainers: Hi, baby! Right here! Come on!

00:06:41.419 --> 00:06:43.080
Narrator: But it can get even more complex

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:44.210
for some actors.

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:46.550
In preparation for a Kind bar commercial,

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:48.610
this crow learned to fly a package

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:50.435
about 12 feet to the porch,

00:06:50.435 --> 00:06:51.740
leave the package in a bowl,

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:52.740
give a little jog,

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:54.610
then fly back out to its crate.

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:57.000
For teaching this type
of sequence of commands,

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.400
Benay uses a concept called back-chaining.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:00.405
That means she'll teach

00:07:00.405 --> 00:07:02.571
the last behavior in a sequence first,

00:07:02.571 --> 00:07:04.410
so flying back from the porch,

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:06.820
and then work backwards
to get the first step,

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:09.253
which is bringing the
package to the porch.

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:14.020
This is Crystal, a capuchin monkey

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:16.648
who's appeared in a long
string of movies and shows

00:07:16.648 --> 00:07:19.500
after her breakout role
in "George of the Jungle."

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:21.091
Her trainer, Tom Gunderson,

00:07:21.091 --> 00:07:22.660
estimates that she recognizes

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:24.460
60 different words in English,

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:26.143
including complex commands like

00:07:26.143 --> 00:07:28.180
"hands on head," "open your mouth,"

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:30.290
"bare your teeth," and
"stick out your tongue."

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:31.123
Director: Action.

00:07:31.123 --> 00:07:32.497
Trainer: Touch. Open.
[Crystal squeaks]

00:07:32.497 --> 00:07:34.010
Bang.

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:36.570
Narrator: In one of the biggest
challenges of her career,

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:38.650
Crystal even learned to pretend-puff

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.320
a fake ceramic cigarette

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:41.917
for "The Hangover Part II."

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:45.440
The smoke was digitally added later on.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:47.120
Despite this extensive training,

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:48.890
Crystal still isn't potty-trained.

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:51.103
Her character in "The
Hangover" wore a costume,

00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:52.570
which conveniently hid the diaper

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:53.890
she had on underneath.

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:56.100
But when Crystal plays a wild monkey,

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:57.630
like in "Failure to Launch"

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:00.350
or "Night at the Museum,"
she can't wear diapers,

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:03.310
which means things can get messier.

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:04.970
She actually pooped on Robin Williams

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:06.740
when they were filming
"Night at the Museum."

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:08.803
Luckily, incidents like that haven't hurt

00:08:08.803 --> 00:08:10.245
Crystal's rep in the industry.

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:14.180
[snake hisses]

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:15.660
A movie like "Snakes on a Plane"

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:19.450
required a snake wrangler as
experienced as Jules Sylvester,

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:23.130
who's handled reptiles
for over 350 movies.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:24.787
Jules refers to what he does

00:08:24.787 --> 00:08:27.550
as "reptile management"
rather than "training."

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:29.005
He says, "You can't make a snake

00:08:29.005 --> 00:08:30.988
do anything they don't want to do."

00:08:30.988 --> 00:08:34.680
What you can do is tap into
the snake's innate behaviors.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:38.030
Snakes generally like to
move from warm to cool spaces

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:39.510
and from light to dark,

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:41.600
so wranglers can direct a snake's movement

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:43.780
by manipulating the
temperature and lighting.

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:46.110
Plus, if you put any
ground-running species,

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:47.250
like a corn snake,

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.740
on an elevated place,
like an airplane seat,

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:51.490
it will make its way down to the ground.

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:54.520
Others, like rat snakes
and pythons, like to climb,

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:56.800
so they'll seek out higher surfaces.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.210
Jules took advantage of
these natural inclinations

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:02.020
to create some chaotic
shots in "Snakes on a Plane"

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:04.970
where snakes are moving
in different directions.

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:07.210
The trainers at Universal
Animals in Atlanta

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:09.101
can handle some nonvenomous snakes

00:09:09.101 --> 00:09:10.070
with their bare hands

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:11.810
or with the help of a snake hook.

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:13.640
That's what they're using
to get some movement

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:15.560
out of a bull snake named Victor

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:16.844
for a recreated scene

00:09:16.844 --> 00:09:19.920
in the Discovery
docuseries "Deadly Cults."

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.210
It's a different ball game
dealing with venomous snakes,

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:23.668
like this timber rattlesnake

00:09:23.668 --> 00:09:24.992
that's getting its venom milked

00:09:24.992 --> 00:09:26.600
for a scene in "MacGyver."

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:29.080
Protective equipment usually
includes shin guards,

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:32.872
fang-proof gloves, and
3-foot-long snake tongs.

00:09:34.819 --> 00:09:36.050
[squeaking]

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:38.650
To train a rat, you have
to get to know it first.

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:40.830
Just ask animal trainer Mark Harden,

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:44.140
who prepared 300 rats for
"Bram Stoker's Dracula"

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:46.360
and 600 of them for "Willard."

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:49.667
Mark casts his rats by observing each one

00:09:49.667 --> 00:09:51.760
for its strengths and weaknesses.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:53.670
He then sorts and tags the rats

00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:55.310
into smaller training groups

00:09:55.310 --> 00:09:56.660
according to their specialty,

00:09:56.660 --> 00:09:58.063
whether it's running, jumping,

00:09:58.063 --> 00:09:59.600
climbing, or biting.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:02.770
In "Willard," six rats played
the character of Socrates,

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:05.800
each one swapping in depending
on the scene's needs.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:08.860
One of the six was
particularly tame and stable,

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:10.583
so Mark used that one in scenes

00:10:10.583 --> 00:10:12.620
where an actor had to handle Socrates.

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:14.190
Another rat was a great runner,

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:15.556
so Mark used it for scenes

00:10:15.556 --> 00:10:17.040
where Socrates was on the run.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:19.270
And for when Socrates had to bite objects,

00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:21.540
like this newspaper or these tires,

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:23.577
Mark chose a rat that liked to chew.

00:10:23.577 --> 00:10:26.000
Then he he'd put food right
by the target object --

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:27.633
typically his own special mixture

00:10:27.633 --> 00:10:29.333
of nut butter and food coloring,

00:10:29.333 --> 00:10:32.150
which creates an edible
paste that can cover props.

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:33.920
One challenge of working with rats

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:35.990
is that they're very tactile creatures

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:38.530
who are sensitive to
new smells and sounds.

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:40.192
According to Mark, "The first smell

00:10:40.192 --> 00:10:42.250
that wafts through that
they don't recognize,

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:43.310
they shut down."

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:45.902
So trainers have to anticipate
the conditions on set

00:10:45.902 --> 00:10:49.252
and recreate them as
closely as possible at home.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:53.606
Trainer Melissa Millett

00:10:53.606 --> 00:10:55.581
and animal coordinator Kirk Jarrett

00:10:55.581 --> 00:10:57.473
have worked with all types of animals,

00:10:57.473 --> 00:11:00.880
but they say cats are the
most difficult to train.

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:02.392
To prepare their cats to star

00:11:02.392 --> 00:11:04.888
in the remake of Stephen
King's "Pet Sematary,"

00:11:04.888 --> 00:11:07.755
Melissa and Kirk used a
technique called targeting,

00:11:07.755 --> 00:11:09.970
where you teach a cat to
touch its nose to an object

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:11.520
by luring them with a treat.

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:14.030
You then have the cat move
to a mark on the ground,

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:16.010
starting with a mark
that's closer to the cat

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:18.430
then little by little
moving it further away.

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:21.270
Another technique they used
is called free-shaping.

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:24.060
Melissa describes this as
more of a guessing game.

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:25.440
You put a mark down on the floor

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:28.048
and basically wait for the
cat to figure out what to do.

00:11:28.048 --> 00:11:30.220
When the cat finds the
mark, they get a treat.

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:32.888
This works nicely for cats
in particular because,

00:11:32.888 --> 00:11:35.305
Melissa says, "They think
they're training me."

00:11:35.305 --> 00:11:36.713
[cat meows]

00:11:39.439 --> 00:11:41.787
The most difficult
thing to get a dog to do

00:11:41.787 --> 00:11:42.770
is nothing.

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:44.700
That's according to Teresa Ann Miller,

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:47.400
the dog trainer for "The
Art of Racing in the Rain,"

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:50.130
a movie told entirely from
a dog's point of view.

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:52.450
Dogs react to a lot of different stimuli,

00:11:52.450 --> 00:11:54.680
even things as small as lights or shadows

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:56.200
moving on the surface of the floor.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:58.740
That's where a feed stick becomes handy.

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:00.400
It's a stick with a treat attached

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:02.020
and a bright green ball on it,

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:04.690
which gives the dog a
clear point to focus on.

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:07.090
Of course, trainers
sometimes have to teach dogs

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:08.950
to be on their worst behavior.

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:10.660
Digging, jumping up on people,

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:12.450
knocking things over, you name it.

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:14.020
Like you see this dog, Shelby,

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:16.724
doing for her starring
role in "A Dog's Way Home."

00:12:16.724 --> 00:12:19.920
On set, the dog's trainer will
often be just out of shot,

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:22.405
giving verbal commands and
holding up a feed stick

00:12:22.405 --> 00:12:24.310
or other reward as incentive.

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:25.980
In "John Wick 3," for example,

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:27.105
when we see Keanu Reeves

00:12:27.105 --> 00:12:28.746
in the back of the cab with the dog,

00:12:28.746 --> 00:12:30.430
the dog's trainer was lying down

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:31.830
on the floor of the car,

00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:33.110
overseeing the action.

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:34.800
But dogs aren't just in it for the food.

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:36.990
They're receptive to rewards of all kinds,

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:39.620
including general
excitement, and, of course,

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:41.271
pets and cuddles.

00:12:45.586 --> 00:12:46.736
Benay: Dance.

00:12:47.677 --> 00:12:48.510
[clicker clicks]

